---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/docs.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Wait all and Wait any — Querial">
    <div class="space-y-8">
        <div>
            <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-querial-indigo-on-dark">For Developers</p>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mt-1">Wait all and Wait any</h1>
            <p class="text-muted-foreground mt-2">
                How a fan-in step decides it is Ready. Informal names
                <em>when-all</em> and <em>when-any</em> mean the same two modes.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none space-y-6 [&>p]:text-muted-foreground [&>h2]:text-foreground [&>h3]:text-foreground">
            <p>
                Join mode lives on the <strong class="text-foreground">consumer</strong>
                — the step that has two or more inbound edges. It is not a separate wait
                node, diamond, or empty gate. The designer shows a Join control on that
                existing node (inspector plus a compact ALL / ANY badge). Roots and
                single-parent steps ignore the setting; they always behave as Wait all.
            </p>
            <p>
                Each inbound edge still answers “what does this predecessor owe me?”
                (<code class="text-foreground">succeeded</code>,
                <code class="text-foreground">completed</code>, or
                <code class="text-foreground">artifact_available</code>). Join mode
                answers “how do those inbound edges combine?” YAML and the package
                field are <code class="text-foreground">join_mode: all</code> or
                <code class="text-foreground">join_mode: any</code>. Omit the field
                and it is Wait all.
            </p>

            <h2>Wait all (default)</h2>
            <p>
                Every inbound edge must be satisfied before the consumer becomes Ready.
                This is the AND join. Data fan-in — header extract plus line extract into
                DuckDB or Staged Database SQL — is always Wait all.
            </p>
            <pre class="rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-muted/40 p-4 text-sm overflow-x-auto"><code>load_sales_order  ──succeeded──┐
                               ├──► reconcile_orders   Wait all
load_sales_line   ──succeeded──┘</code></pre>
            <p>
                If any parent that is required to succeed fails, is skipped, is
                cancelled, or is abandoned, the consumer is skipped immediately. It
                does not wait for the remaining parents.
            </p>

            <h2>Wait any</h2>
            <p>
                At least one inbound edge must be satisfied. This is the OR join.
                The first winner opens the gate. Unsatisfied siblings
                <strong class="text-foreground">keep running</strong> — opening Wait any
                does not skip, cancel, or abandon losing branches.
            </p>
            <pre class="rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-muted/40 p-4 text-sm overflow-x-auto"><code>extract_primary    ──succeeded──┐
                                ├──► notify_source_ready   Wait any
extract_fallback   ──succeeded──┘</code></pre>
            <p>
                <code class="text-foreground">notify_source_ready</code> becomes Ready
                when the first extract succeeds. The other extract continues. If every
                parent finishes terminal and none satisfied the edge, the consumer is
                skipped with reason <code class="text-foreground">wait_any_unsatisfied</code>.
                While a loser is still running, the consumer stays pending — a failed
                parent is “not yet a winner,” not an immediate skip.
            </p>
            <p>
                Edge requirement still matters. With
                <code class="text-foreground">succeeded</code> edges, a failed parent
                does not open the gate; a sibling success can. With
                <code class="text-foreground">completed</code> edges, the first parent
                that reaches any terminal status satisfies the OR. A parent in
                <code class="text-foreground">retry_waiting</code> is neither succeeded
                nor completed; a different parent that already succeeded can still
                open the gate.
            </p>

            <h2>Wait any is control-only</h2>
            <p>
                Publish, package validation, and the draft Join save reject
                <code class="text-foreground">join_mode: any</code> when any of these
                is true:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
                <li>The step has fewer than two inbound edges.</li>
                <li>The handler is <code class="text-foreground">artifact-sql</code> or <code class="text-foreground">staged-database-sql</code>.</li>
                <li>Step configuration binds <code class="text-foreground">from_step</code> (including a list of producers).</li>
                <li>Any inbound edge is <code class="text-foreground">artifact_available</code>.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Those shapes are data joins. They must wait for every input. There is
                no “take the first Parquet” winner binding. Allowed Wait any consumers
                in v1 are classic SQL handlers and
                <code class="text-foreground">database-query-to-parquet</code> when
                they have no <code class="text-foreground">from_step</code> — for
                example a notify or validation step after two alternative extracts.
            </p>
            <p>
                The designer disables Wait any on Artifact SQL and Staged Database SQL.
                If you drop a parent so inbound count falls below two, the draft is
                coerced back to Wait all. Publish still errors if
                <code class="text-foreground">any</code> remains on a node with fewer
                than two inbound edges.
            </p>

            <h2>Where you set it</h2>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
                <li>DAG designer inspector, only when the selected node has two or more inbound edges.</li>
                <li>Step create/edit modal Join select (same rule; disabled below two inbound edges).</li>
                <li>Package YAML: omit for Wait all; write <code class="text-foreground">join_mode: any</code> only when you mean it. Export omits Wait all so existing hashes stay stable.</li>
            </ul>
            <pre class="rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-muted/40 p-4 text-sm overflow-x-auto"><code>steps:
  - code: notify_source_ready
    type: sql_command
    join_mode: any</code></pre>
            <p>
                There is no pipeline-level default join mode and no extra feature flag.
                Join mode is DAG execution only. While legacy serial execution is still
                on, the pipeline runs by order and
                <code class="text-foreground">join_mode</code> is unused.
            </p>

            <h2>Runs, fail-fast, and retry</h2>
            <p>
                When a run plan is created, join mode is copied onto each step
                execution. Later draft edits do not change an in-flight run. If inbound
                count at plan time is below two, the snapshot is Wait all.
            </p>
            <p>
                Run-wide <strong class="text-foreground">fail fast</strong> still wins.
                A mandatory (<code class="text-foreground">fail_pipeline</code>) failure
                latches skip of remaining unstarted work, including a pending Wait any
                consumer, before OR logic can start it. Losing siblings that are already
                running are not cancelled by Wait any itself; fail-fast cancel-in-flight
                is the separate run policy.
            </p>
            <p>
                Failed-cone recovery does not need a special join selector. A Wait any
                consumer that already succeeded is reused. Parents that failed remain
                in the failed cone. The run visualizer shows the same ALL / ANY badges
                as the designer.
            </p>
            <p>
                Operators: see
                <a href="/docs/devops-scheduling" class="text-link hover:underline">Scheduling and runs</a>
                and
                <a href="/docs/devops-retry" class="text-link hover:underline">Retry and failed-cone recovery</a>.
                In-run policy lives on
                <a href="/docs/developer-retry" class="text-link hover:underline">Step retry policies</a>.
                Related design pages:
                <a href="/docs/developer-pipelines" class="text-link hover:underline">Pipelines and the DAG designer</a>,
                <a href="/docs/developer-packages" class="text-link hover:underline">Packages</a>,
                <a href="/docs/devops-artifacts" class="text-link hover:underline">Artifacts</a>.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</DocsLayout>
